+		<English>[TAB]Among the Lakota, traditional law enforcers known as akicitas came from men's societies and were appointed by a band's camp administrator. They policed camp movements, regulated communal buffalo hunts, and enforced tribal laws and customs. When dealing with lawbreakers, akicitas could perform judicial as well as policing duties.[PARAGRAPH:1]Akicita societies also trained warriors and participated in hunts and warfare, joining military responsibility with the maintenance of order within the community. Sitting Bull rose to prominence as a hunter and war leader and belonged to several prestigious warrior societies.[PARAGRAPH:1]The Soldiers' Lodge represents these connected warrior, policing, judicial, and community institutions.[PARAGRAPH:1]Sources: https://plainshumanities.unl.edu/encyclopedia/doc/egp.law.022.html; https://www.sdhspress.com/journal/south-dakota-history-29-3/reservation-akicitas-the-pine-ridge-indian-police-1879-1885/vol-29-no-3-reservation-akicitas.pdf</English>
